# SPRING BOOT 4 / JACKSON 3 CONSTRAINTS (Java files only — skip for TypeScript/TSX)
This project uses Spring Boot 4.x. Several APIs changed from Spring Boot 2/3. These rules
override anything the instruction implies — apply them unconditionally to every Java file.

JACKSON IMPORTS — groupId changed. Never write com.fasterxml.jackson.*:
  tools.jackson.databind.ObjectMapper      (NOT com.fasterxml.jackson.databind.ObjectMapper)
  tools.jackson.databind.JsonNode          (NOT com.fasterxml.jackson.databind.JsonNode)
  tools.jackson.annotation.JsonProperty   (NOT com.fasterxml.jackson.annotation.JsonProperty)
  tools.jackson.core.JacksonException     (NOT JsonProcessingException — it was renamed)
Any import starting with com.fasterxml.jackson is wrong. Replace with tools.jackson.

EXCEPTION CLASSES — Spring Boot 4 ships NO application-level exceptions:
  ResourceNotFoundException, PaymentGatewayException, etc. must be separate .java files
  in the .exception package. Never declare them inline. If you reference one that is not
  in the dependency files, add the correct import (com.{pkg}.exception.ClassName) — the
  standalone file will be generated; do NOT inline it as a nested class.

JPA BIDIRECTIONAL SAVE — set the child's owning-side reference BEFORE saving the parent.
  When a parent owns a @OneToMany(cascade=…) collection whose child holds the @ManyToOne +
  @JoinColumn(nullable=false), populating parent.setChildren(list) and then repo.save(parent)
  inserts every child with a NULL foreign key and the request 500s at runtime. Always set the
  back-reference first, e.g.:
      order.setItems(orderItems);
      orderItems.forEach(i -> i.setOrder(order));   // REQUIRED before the save
      orderRepository.save(order);                  // cascade now writes a valid order_id

JPA SEEDING — never assign an @Id that is @GeneratedValue. In a CommandLineRunner/seeder,
  construct each entity with the no-arg constructor + setters (or a builder) and leave the id
  null so Hibernate generates it. Passing an explicit id (e.g. new Trainer(UUID.randomUUID(), …))
  makes Spring Data see a non-null id, treat the row as detached, and issue an UPDATE (merge) that
  matches zero rows — the seeder crashes at boot with ObjectOptimisticLockingFailureException and
  the app restart-loops. Seed every entity the way its siblings do: no id argument.

PRINCIPAL IS NOT AN ID — @AuthenticationPrincipal UserDetails.getUsername() is the login subject
  (the email/username), NOT a UUID or numeric id. Never UUID.fromString(userDetails.getUsername())
  or otherwise parse it as an entity id. Resolve the user through the repository
  (userRepository.findByEmail(userDetails.getUsername())) and use that entity's id.

8. UI COMPONENTS — shadcn-first. Follow this priority order for frontend files:
   (a) PREFER shadcn: for any element shadcn provides — Button, Input, Textarea, Label, Select,
       Checkbox, RadioGroup, Switch, Form, Dialog, AlertDialog, DropdownMenu, Popover, Tooltip,
       Sheet, Table, Card, Badge, Separator, Tabs, Accordion, Avatar, Skeleton, Calendar, and
       Toast (via sonner) — import from @/components/ui/<name>, ALWAYS lowercase
       (@/components/ui/select, NEVER @/components/ui/Select). These files are pre-installed; the
       UI INVENTORY section lists each one's exact exports — use those names verbatim.
   (b) Radix ONLY as fallback: import from @radix-ui/react-* directly ONLY when a primitive is
       absent from the UI INVENTORY. Never reach for raw radix for anything shadcn already wraps.
   (c) Layout and static elements: use plain HTML tags with Tailwind CSS classes.
   (d) Toast: import { toast } from 'sonner' — never react-toastify or a use-toast hook.
   NEVER import from @/lib/utils in app files. Use clsx for className merging.

# TYPESCRIPT / REACT 19 CONSTRAINTS (TS/TSX files only — every rule fixes a recurring build breaker)
1. TANSTACK QUERY v5 API ONLY (v4 idioms do not compile):
   - useQuery({ queryKey: ['trainers'], queryFn: fetchTrainers })  — OBJECT form; positional
     useQuery(['trainers'], fetchTrainers) is a compile error in v5.
   - Mutations expose isPending — mutation.isLoading DOES NOT EXIST on useMutation results.
   - useMutation({ mutationFn: createTrainer, onSuccess: ... }) — object form here too.
2. ZOD NUMERIC FORM FIELDS: HTML inputs yield strings — use z.coerce.number() (NOT z.number())
   for any numeric field bound to an <input>, or the zodResolver output type will not match
   the DTO interface and the resolver assignment fails to compile.
3. TYPE RE-EXPORTS: isolatedModules is on — re-exporting a type requires
   export type { Foo } from './bar'  (a plain export { Foo } fails to compile).
4. BOOLEAN JSX ATTRIBUTES: allowFullScreen, disabled, checked take booleans —
   allowFullScreen={true} or bare allowFullScreen, never allowFullScreen="true".
5. API TYPES AND SERVICES ARE PRE-GENERATED GROUND TRUTH: frontend/src/types/<domain>.ts
   and frontend/src/services/<domain>Service.ts are DERIVED from the backend contract and
   already exist. Their real content appears in your dependency context: import the
   exact exported interfaces and service functions shown there. ADMIN API functions live
   in the same domain service as the public ones (admin-prefixed on name collisions) —
   there is no adminXService module; the API SDK section of the contract card is the
   complete function list. NEVER call axios/apiClient directly from pages, hooks, or
   components — always call the generated service functions. Frontend-only types that
   have no backend counterpart (cart state, form view-models) go in
   frontend/src/types/local/<name>.ts; non-API service modules (localStorage persistence)
   go in frontend/src/services/local/<name>.ts.

   THE DERIVED FILES WIN OVER THE INSTRUCTION — for NAMES and for LOCATIONS alike. The
   instruction was written before these files were derived, so wherever it contradicts a
   types/services file in your dependency context, the DERIVED FILE is correct and the
   instruction is stale.
   (a) FIELD AND TYPE NAMES: if the instruction says a card shows `durationMonths` but the
       type declares `durationInMonths`, write durationInMonths; if it names a field the type
       does not declare (e.g. features), that field DOES NOT EXIST — omit it, or render from
       fields that do exist.
   (b) WHICH MODULE EXPORTS A FUNCTION: the instruction may say "bookingService exports
       getAllFitnessClasses" while the SDK card shows classService exporting it. The CARD is
       correct. Import each symbol from the module that actually exports it — splitting one
       instructed import across several modules when that is where the functions live. If a
       named module does not export what the instruction claims, find the module that does;
       NEVER hand-write an apiClient/axios call to work around the missing export, and never
       invent an endpoint path from a DTO name.
   (c) Fields typed `T | null` must be null-guarded (value ?? fallback) before use in
       non-null positions.

   IMPORT ONLY MODULES THAT APPEAR IN YOUR DEPENDENCY CONTEXT: never invent an import path
   like '@/types/gymClass' because the instruction prose mentions "GymClass" — if that
   module is not among the dependency files shown to you, the type lives in one of the
   modules that IS shown (check their exports). An import of a non-existent module is an
   unfixable compile error.

6. IMPORT DIRECTION — NO CIRCULAR IMPORTS: imports flow ONE WAY, down the layer stack:
   PAGE → COMPONENT → HOOK → CONTEXT → SERVICE → types. A file may import only from a layer
   BELOW it. NEVER the reverse: a COMPONENT must not import a PAGE, a HOOK must not import a
   COMPONENT or PAGE. Two files in the SAME layer must not import each other — sibling
   components communicate through props passed by their shared parent, and shared data types
   live in frontend/src/types/. A cycle (A imports B, B imports A, directly or through a chain)
   builds clean under tsc and vite but crashes at RUNTIME with "Cannot access '<name>' before
   initialization" and renders a blank page — it is caught by a dedicated gate and fails the
   whole build, so do not create one.

7. ROUTES ARE GROUND TRUTH: the ROUTE MANIFEST section in context lists the COMPLETE route
   set, derived from the plan into frontend/src/routes.ts. Every <Link to=...> and
   navigate(...) target MUST be ROUTES.<KEY> imported from '@/routes' — NEVER a bare path
   string literal, and never a route absent from the manifest (it does not exist). Nav
   rendering comes from the same file: Header/public nav maps
   routeTable.filter(r => r.nav && !r.admin); AdminLayout's sidebar maps
   routeTable.filter(r => r.nav && r.admin) — never hardcode nav links. routes.ts imports
   nothing, so importing it can never create a cycle.
8. LOCAL IMPORTS ARE A CLOSED SET — THE MODULE CATALOG IS EXHAUSTIVE: when a "MODULES THAT
   ALREADY EXIST" section is present in your dependency context, it lists every local module
   generated so far, one line per module (@/path -> exported names). For any '@/...' import,
   BOTH the module path AND the named symbol MUST appear there — import them exactly as shown.
   A local module or symbol absent from that list DOES NOT EXIST: never import it, never infer
   its path from a type/component name in the instruction, and never create the missing file.
   If the section is absent, no local app modules exist yet. Either way, when the instruction
   implies a local helper/type/component the catalog does not list, define it inline in THIS
   file — a frontend-only type goes in frontend/src/types/local/<name>.ts. This governs only
   '@/'-aliased app modules — real npm packages (react, @tanstack/react-query, lucide-react,
   @radix-ui/*) and the shadcn files in the AVAILABLE UI IMPORTS list are unaffected. An
   import of a non-existent local module is an unfixable compile error.
9. AUTH STATE HYDRATES SYNCHRONOUSLY: a context that restores a session from localStorage must
   seed state with lazy useState initializers — useState(() => localStorage.getItem('token')) and
   useState(() => !!localStorage.getItem('token')) — NEVER useState(null) + a useEffect that reads
   localStorage. A useEffect runs AFTER the first render, so on a refresh or deep-link of a
   protected route ProtectedRoute reads isAuthenticated=false on first paint and redirects to
   /login, silently logging the user out.
10. READ THE ID FROM WHERE THE URL PUTS IT: match the param reader to the route definition in the
   ROUTE MANIFEST. If navigation targets a query string (navigate(`/checkout?planId=${id}`) with
   <Route path="/checkout">), read useSearchParams().get('planId') — useParams() returns {}
   because the route declares no :param. Only use useParams() when the route is path-parameterised
   (<Route path="/checkout/:id">). A mismatch fetches with an empty id, loads no data, and renders
   the error/empty state.

# UI QUALITY STANDARDS (PAGE and COMPONENT files only — skip for Java/config files)
1. LAYOUT WRAPPER: public Page.tsx wraps JSX in <Layout> from @/components/Layout. Admin*Page.tsx wraps in <AdminLayout> from @/components/AdminLayout. Never inline nav or sidebar inside a page.
2. SECTION CONTAINERS: <section className="py-16 px-4"><div className="max-w-7xl mx-auto">…</div></section> — content never touches viewport edge.
3. RESPONSIVE GRIDS: grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3 — never fixed column counts on mobile.
4. RESPONSIVE TYPOGRAPHY: hero h1 = text-4xl md:text-6xl font-bold; section headings = text-2xl md:text-3xl font-semibold.
5. HERO SECTIONS: relative h-[500px] md:h-[600px] bg-cover bg-center flex items-center justify-center + <div className="absolute inset-0 bg-black bg-opacity-50" /> overlay.
6. HOVER/TRANSITIONS: every clickable element has hover: class + transition-all duration-200.
7. LOADING STATES: data-fetching components render a skeleton or spinner while isLoading is true — never an empty container.
8. EMPTY STATES: when a list/result is empty, render a styled empty-state with icon + message — not a blank section.
9. DYNAMIC COPY ONLY: business name, phone, address, hours — all from props or API response. Zero hardcoded strings in JSX.
10. MOBILE NAV: a Header/navbar that hides its links with `hidden md:flex` MUST provide a mobile fallback — a hamburger button (lucide-react Menu/X, shown `md:hidden`) that toggles a dropdown listing the same links plus the auth actions. Without it, mobile visitors see only the logo and cannot navigate.
11. TEST HOOKS (data-testid): put a `data-testid` on every element an automated flow must find — so browser e2e tests locate elements by a stable id, not brittle text. Use kebab-case with a stable convention: nav links `nav-<routeKey>` (nav-memberships); a form `<entity>-form`; its inputs `<entity>-<field>` (login-email, login-password, trial-name); its submit `<entity>-submit` (login-submit); primary CTAs `<action>-cta` (join-cta, book-cta); repeated list/card items `<entity>-card` (same testid on each — tests select by index). Add them to real interactive elements only (inputs, buttons, links, cards); never to layout wrappers. This is additive — it never changes rendered output or replaces accessibility roles/labels.

# OUTPUT
Return ONLY raw file content — no fences, language tag, explanation, or preamble.
First character = first character of the file; last character = last character of the file.

# SELF-CHECK (do not output)
- Only what was specified — zero added members, imports, logic, or comments?
- Every member matches name, type, modifier, signature exactly?
- All references match the instruction/files; nothing fabricated?
- For PAGE files with forms: does every zod field type produce the TypeScript type that
  matches the corresponding service interface field? (z.number() → number, z.string() →
  string; z.string().optional() → string | undefined). No String() casts or ?? '' patches
  should be needed in onSubmit() if the schema is correct.
- Compiles as-is? Output is raw with no fences or surrounding text?

